      <section class="article-section">
        <h2>The Purpose and Architecture of House Rent Allowance in India</h2>
        <p>
          In the compensation structure of the Central Government of India, <strong>House Rent Allowance (HRA)</strong> serves a specialized socio-economic purpose. Unlike basic pay, which compensates qualifications, responsibility, and operational cadre, HRA is a location-specific compensatory grant designed to offset the cost of securing private residential accommodation when official residential quarters—such as General Pool Residential Accommodation (GPRA), railway quarters, or defence cantonment housing—are unavailable.
        </p>
        <p>
          Residential real estate rental dynamics vary widely across the country. An employee stationed in Mumbai, New Delhi, or Bengaluru incurs significantly higher rental expenditures for equivalent living space than a colleague stationed in a district tehsil or rural border outpost. 
        </p>
        <p>
          To manage these regional disparities, successive Central Pay Commissions have relied on a systematic <strong>city categorization framework</strong>. With the implementation of the <strong>8th Central Pay Commission (8th CPC)</strong>, HRA rates, percentage thresholds, and minimum statutory floor guarantees will undergo decisive revisions to align with contemporary urban rental markets.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="article-section">
        <h2>City Classification Slabs: Class X, Y, and Z Categorization Rules</h2>
        <p>
          The classification of cities for HRA is determined by official population metrics derived from the decennial Census of India, overseen by the Ministry of Urban Development and the Department of Expenditure (DoE):
        </p>

        <div class="category-grid">
          <div class="category-card tier-x">
            <h4>Class X (Tier-1 Metropolitan Areas)</h4>
            <div class="badge-tag">Population: 50 Lakh and Above</div>
            <p><strong>Baseline HRA Slabs:</strong> 24% to 27% (Projected up to 30% under peak indexation).</p>
            <p><strong>Major Urban Centers:</strong> Delhi NCR (including Noida, Gurugram, Ghaziabad, Faridabad), Greater Mumbai, Kolkata, Chennai, Bengaluru, Hyderabad, Ahmedabad, and Pune.</p>
            <span class="card-footer-note">Represents the highest urban real estate cost tier in India.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="category-card tier-y">
            <h4>Class Y (Tier-2 Urban Centers)</h4>
            <div class="badge-tag">Population: 5 Lakh to 50 Lakh</div>
            <p><strong>Baseline HRA Slabs:</strong> 18% to 20%.</p>
            <p><strong>Major Urban Centers:</strong> State capitals and major industrial cities including Lucknow, Jaipur, Patna, Bhopal, Chandigarh, Kochi, Visakhapatnam, Coimbatore, Indore, Nagpur, and Vadodara.</p>
            <span class="card-footer-note">Covers municipal corporation limits and major satellite urban agglomerations.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="category-card tier-z">
            <h4>Class Z (Tier-3 Municipalities & Rural Outposts)</h4>
            <div class="badge-tag">Population: Below 5 Lakh</div>
            <p><strong>Baseline HRA Slabs:</strong> 9% to 10%.</p>
            <p><strong>Covered Areas:</strong> All small towns, rural taluk headquarters, non-classified military/railway stations, and remote administrative locations.</p>
            <span class="card-footer-note">Baseline rate ensuring minimum rental compensation across all non-metro areas.</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

