      <section class="article-section">
        <h2>Statutory Rules on Dual-Government Spouses & Accommodation</h2>
        <p>
          A frequent operational question involves HRA admissibility when both husband and wife are government servants:
        </p>
        <ul class="editorial-list">
          <li><strong>When Government Accommodation Is Allotted:</strong> Under Fundamental Rule (FR) 45 and Supplementary Rules (SR), if either spouse is allotted official government residential quarters (GPRA) at the same station, <em>neither spouse is entitled to draw House Rent Allowance</em>.</li>
          <li><strong>When Living in Private Rented Housing:</strong> If both spouses reside in privately rented accommodation at the same station, <em>both are individually entitled to draw their respective full HRA</em> as per their individual pay scales.</li>
          <li><strong>Separate Duty Stations:</strong> If husband and wife are posted at different administrative stations, each draws HRA based on their individual posting city classification, regardless of whether one occupies official quarters at their respective post.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="editorial-chapter">
        <h2>Impact on State Government Employees (Tamil Nadu, UP, Maharashtra & Beyond)</h2>
        <p>
          While the Central Government mandates HRA classifications for central civil servants, postal staff, and defence personnel, state administrations across <strong>Tamil Nadu</strong>, <strong>Uttar Pradesh</strong>, <strong>Maharashtra</strong>, <strong>Rajasthan</strong>, <strong>Bihar</strong>, and <strong>Karnataka</strong> adapt these structures through their respective State Pay Revision Committees.
        </p>
        <p>
          State finance departments evaluate central guidelines and release localized Government Orders (G.O.) mapping state cities—such as classifying Chennai, Lucknow, Mumbai, and Bengaluru into Tier-1 brackets and district headquarters into Tier-2 or Tier-3 brackets. Consequently, the calculations provided by our HRA tool serve as a critical analytical roadmap for millions of civil servants, police personnel, and government teachers across India.
        </p>
      </section>

